# Shell scripts run on Linux and macOS. A CRLF checkout makes every one of
# them fail at the shebang with "bad interpreter: bash^M", so they are LF
# whatever the platform and whatever core.autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Recorded acceptance runs are evidence: their bytes are hashed by their
# own manifests and rechecked by `hardy accept --recorded`, so no line-ending
# conversion may touch them on any platform.
acceptance/recorded/** -text
# Evals evidence is bound by digests over its bytes: the problem list and
# tier file are hashed into every scoreboard, and every scoreboard's rows
# point at run directories whose bytes its own record hashes. No line-ending
# conversion may touch any of it on any platform.
evals/** -text
# The corpus is bound the same way: `manifest_digest` hashes every content
# file's raw bytes and the changelog head binds that digest, so a CRLF
# checkout would make a published version verify clean nowhere.
corpus/** -text
# `npm run build` (Vite) reads `web/index.html` as its template and emits
# `src/hardy/app/web/static/index.html`. On Windows, with the ordinary
# `core.autocrlf=true` and no rule here, the *source* template checks out
# CRLF; Vite's HTML transform then re-serialises most lines with its own
# line ending while leaving a `\r` from the source embedded ahead of it, so
# the built file comes out with a mix of LF, CRLF and, on at least one line,
# `\r\r\n` -- a sequence git's own CRLF normalisation cannot fully collapse,
# so the output stayed dirty even once forced to `eol=lf` on its own. CI
# (`ubuntu-latest`) never saw any of this. Forcing the *source* template to
# LF removes the stray `\r` at the root, and forcing the built file to LF
# too is the belt the issue asked for -- together a rebuild reproduces
# exactly what is committed, on every platform.
web/index.html text eol=lf
src/hardy/app/web/static/index.html text eol=lf
